{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `dd9a83f7`: both accepted.\n\nRetention\n\n1. The store keeps the authorization history of a key while the\n   store lives. E4 adds no deletion and no eviction.\n2. A key holds at most 64 gesture positions. An unsigned position\n   and a failed position each count. The number equals\n   `SIGNINGS_MOST` in `custody.ts`, and the note states that the two\n   bounds are separate constants.\n3. A gesture past the bound stops before any write, under one stop\n   name that the note states. The member continues with a new\n   request key.\n4. Custody keeps a terminal record in its archive, so an archived\n   record can still name its authorization. A removal rule belongs\n   to the step that builds a durable authorization store. That step\n   states it with the removal rule of the custody archive. E4\n   delivers the port and the memory store only.\n\nOrder\n\n5. Yes. After the corrected source passes its review, make a\n   private E4 branch at exact `5784e23d`. Apply the source, and run\n   the affected compiler contexts, the whole owners and the two\n   controls.\n6. The branch changes no identifier file and no version file until\n   Lending 0.4.0 and Counter 0.4.0 are signed. The release\n   reproduction owner fails on that branch for this reason. Run no\n   `npm run pins` and no gate there before the signing, and report\n   no such failure as a finding.\n7. Run nothing of E4 while a gate or a merge of the three approved\n   heads runs.\n8. After the signing I send the version decision. By ruling\n   `163de663` the first change after a signing opens the next\n   minor version. Then the identifiers, one full gate, the review\n   and the landing follow.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}